# SYNOPSIS

```text
block 0           superblock
block 1 ...       arena 0: header, data blocks, footer
                  arena 1 ... arena n-1
                  (unused)
last block        superblock, a copy
```

# DESCRIPTION

vx-fs is the system volume's file system, after 9front's gefs: the volume is a forest of copy-on-write Bε trees in blocks of one size, and a file system is one tree's keys.
fsd(4) serves a volume, install(8) makes one, and vxfs(8) makes and reads images on the development machine; all three use `lib/vx-fs`, and nothing else reads or writes the format.
The volume is a partition, or an image file holding one.

Every block is 16384 bytes and starts at a byte address that is a multiple of 16384.
Integers in blocks are little-endian.
Integers inside keys are big-endian, so that keys compare as bytes and sort as their numbers do.
The hash is XXH64, with seed 0 unless this page says otherwise; it catches failing media and bugs, not tampering.
Times are signed nanoseconds since 1970, UTC, as the system's clock gives them (counted from boot before it has a wall clock).

A generation is a number from one counter, kept in the superblock, which also numbers snapshots.
Each tree is changed in a generation of its own, which ends at a commit; every block a tree writes carries the generation it was born in, and a snapshot's id is the generation of its newest blocks.
0 means none.

Nothing written is changed in place, except an arena's log, its header and footer, and the superblocks.
A block is reached through a pointer holding its hash, so a block read wrongly is found and refused, and the volume is then read-only; every offset and length inside a block is checked too, so a damaged volume makes errors, never faults.

The page describes each structure as the code lays it out: the superblock, arenas and their logs, block pointers, the trees' blocks, keys and messages, the snapshot tree, and files and directories.
A field is written `name[n]`, <n> its size in bytes.

@node=superblock title="The superblock"

The superblock is the volume's first block, and a copy is its last.
A commit writes the first, then, after a barrier, the copy, so a power cut tears at most one of them.
Mounting reads both, takes the whole one with the larger `commit`, or the other if its arenas will not load, and rewrites the other copy before it writes anything else.

| Offset | Field | Holds |
| 0 | `magic[4]` | `0x73667876`, the bytes `vxfs` |
| 4 | `version[4]` | 1 |
| 8 | `blksz[4]` | 16384 |
| 12 | `bufspc[4]` | 8187, a pivot's buffer's size |
| 16 | `narenas[4]` | the arenas, 1 to 677 |
| 20 | `snapht[4]` | the snapshot tree's height, 1 to 32 |
| 24 | `snaproot[24]` | a pointer to the snapshot tree's root |
| 48 | `commit[8]` | the commits made so far |
| 56 | `nextgen[8]` | the next generation |
| 64 | `nextqid[8]` | the next `qid.path` |
| 72 | `nextdl[8]` | the next deadlist sequence number |
| 80 | `flags[8]` | 0 |
| 88 | `freed[8]` | the address of the freed chain, or 0 |
| 96 | `spare[8]` | 0 |
| 104 | the arena table | `narenas` entries of 24 bytes |
| 104 + 24 × `narenas` | `hash[8]` | XXH64 of every byte before it |

The rest of the block is zeros.
Each arena table entry is `base[8] blocks[8] hash[8]`: the arena's header's address, its count of data blocks, and the hash of its header block.

A superblock is refused if its magic, version, block size or buffer size differ, if `narenas` is 0 or over 677, if `snapht` is outside 1 to 32, or if its hash does not match.
An arena that does not lie wholly between the two superblocks, or that overlaps another, is refused.

The freed chain lists what the commit freed, in deadlist blocks ({the snapshot tree|#snapshots}).
Those frees were logged after the part of the logs the commit covers, so mounting does them again, and frees the chain's own blocks at the next commit.

@node=arenas title="Arenas and their logs"

An arena is a header block at `base`, `blocks` data blocks after it, and a footer after them: a copy of the header, which a commit writes after the superblock.
Every block of a tree, a file, a log or a chain is a data block of some arena.
A volume made by `lib/vx-fs` has one arena for each 4 GiB, 64 at most and each of at least 64 blocks; arena <i> starts at block 1 + <i> × <per>, where <per> is the volume's blocks less two, divided by the number of arenas, and holds <per> − 2 data blocks.

The header block is:

| Offset | Field | Holds |
| 0 | `type[2]` | 5 |
| 2 | `base[8]` | the header's own address |
| 10 | `blocks[8]` | the data blocks |
| 18 | `loghd[8]` | the address of the log's first block |
| 26 | `logtl[8]` | the address of the log's last block |
| 34 | `tailsz[2]` | the bytes of the last block the commit covers |
| 36 | `tailhash[8]` | XXH64 of those bytes |

and zeros.
Mounting reads the header, checked against the superblock's hash of it, or the footer if the header does not match; its `base` and `blocks` must agree with the table's.

An arena's free space is its log: a chain of log blocks recording each allocation and free, replayed at mount, which is the one structure appended to in place.
A log block is:

| Offset | Field | Holds |
| 0 | `type[2]` | 3 |
| 2 | `logsz[2]` | the bytes of entries, a multiple of 8, at most 16348 |
| 4 | `loghash[8]` | the block's own hash |
| 12 | `next[24]` | a pointer to the next block, its address only |
| 36 | entries | 16348 bytes |

`loghash` is XXH64 of the entries, seeded with XXH64 of the first 4 bytes (seed 0) exclusive-or XXH64 of `next` (seed 1), so a damaged header is found as a damaged entry is.
An entry is a word `addr[8]`, a block's address with an operation in its low byte, which a block-aligned address leaves free:

: 1, one block taken
  `addr` alone.
: 2, one block freed
  `addr` alone.
: 3, blocks taken
  Followed by `len[8]`, the bytes taken from `addr`, a nonzero multiple of 16384.
: 4, blocks freed
  Followed by `len[8]`, likewise.

Any other operation is refused.
A replay starts with nothing free; a new arena's log starts with a free of all its data blocks and the taking of the log's first block.
When a block's entries reach 16308 bytes, the next block is taken, recorded in the full block as a one-block take, and named in its `next`.
A replay reads each block from `loghd` checked by its own hash, then of `logtl` only the `tailsz` bytes the header covers, checked against `tailhash`; the rest of that block was written after the commit and is ignored.
Every block of the log must then be one the log says is taken.
A long log is rewritten as new blocks holding only frees, one for each free range; the old blocks stay taken until the commit naming the new log is durable.

@node=pointers title="Block pointers"

A block pointer is `addr[8] hash[8] gen[8]`:

: `addr`
  The block's byte address on the volume, a multiple of 16384.
: `hash`
  XXH64 of the block's 16384 bytes.
: `gen`
  The generation the block was born in.

A block whose hash does not match its pointer is refused.
Log and deadlist blocks carry their own hash instead, and the pointers to them have only an address.
A block is never older than a block it points at.

@node=blocks title="The trees' blocks"

A block's first two bytes are its type, except a data block's:

: 0, data
  A file's bytes, 16384 of them, with no header.
: 1, pivot
  An inner node of a tree.
: 2, leaf
  A tree's values.
: 3, log
  An arena's log ({arenas|#arenas}).
: 4, deadlist
  A chain of addresses ({the snapshot tree|#snapshots}).
: 5, arena
  An arena's header or footer.

A pivot's header is `type[2] nval[2] valsz[2] nbuf[2] bufsz[2]`, 10 bytes.
After it are two areas of 8187 bytes: its children, then a buffer of messages bound for them.
A leaf's header is `type[2] nval[2] valsz[2]`, 6 bytes, and one area of 16378 bytes, its values, follows.

An area is a table.
At its front are `nval` (or `nbuf`) offsets of 2 bytes, each from the area's start to an entry; the entries are packed from the area's end towards the front, and `valsz` (or `bufsz`) is their bytes, the offsets apart.
An entry is

```text
op[1] nk[2] key[nk] nv[2] value[nv]
```

with `op` only in a buffer.
A key is 1 to 256 bytes, and a value at most 512.
Keys compare byte by byte, and a key that is a prefix of another sorts first.

- In a leaf, the entries are values in strictly increasing order of key.
- In a pivot's first area, each entry is a child: the least key it holds, or less, and a value of 26 bytes, a block pointer and `fill[2]`, the bytes the child uses, its offsets included.
  There is at least one, in strictly increasing order of key.
  A key belongs to the last child whose key is at most it, or to the first if none is.
- In a pivot's buffer, the entries are messages ({keys and messages|#keys}) for its children, in order of key; messages for one key are in the order they were made, oldest first.

A tree's height is 1 when its root is a leaf, and at most 32.
A value is read by applying, to the leaf's value, the messages for its key buffered on the path, the deepest first.

@node=keys title="Keys and messages"

A key's first byte is its type:

: 0, `Kdat qid[8] off[8]`
  A block of a file's data: the file's `qid.path` and the block-aligned offset.
  The value is a kind byte, then, for kind 0, a block pointer to a data block (25 bytes in all), or, for kind 1, the bytes themselves, at most 511, the rest of the block reading as zeros.
: 1, `Kent pqid[8] name[]`
  A directory entry: the directory's `qid.path` and the entry's name.
  The value is the entry ({files and directories|#files}).
: 2, `Kup qid[8]`
  The value is the `Kent` or `Korphan` key of the entry whose `qid.path` is <qid>.
: 3, `Klabel name[]`
  A label, in the snapshot tree.
: 4, `Ksnap id[8]`
  A snapshot, in the snapshot tree.
: 5, `Kdlist snap[8] birth[8] seq[8]`
  A deadlist, in the snapshot tree.
: 6, `Korphan qid[8]`
  A file removed while it was open; the value is its entry.

A message is an operation on a key:

: 1, insert
  The value, replacing any.
: 2, delete
  The key goes.
: 3, clear block
  A `Kdat` key goes, if it is there.
: 4, clobber
  The key goes, if it is there, and nothing is freed.
: 5, wstat
  An entry's fields change in place, and its `qid.vers` grows by one.

Operation 0, or one over 5, is refused.
An insert, delete or clear block that removes a `Kdat` value naming a data block frees that block.
A wstat's value is `flags[1]`, then each field a flag names, in this order, and nothing else:

| Flag | Field |
| 0x01 | `length[8]` |
| 0x02 | `mode[4]`, which also sets `qid.type` to its top byte |
| 0x04 | `mtime[8]` |
| 0x08 | `atime[8]` |
| 0x10 | `uid[4]` |
| 0x20 | `gid[4]` |
| 0x40 | `muid[4]` |
| 0x80 | `ctime[8]` |

A wstat of a value that is not a 77-byte entry is damage.

@node=snapshots title="The snapshot tree"

The superblock's `snaproot` is the snapshot tree, a tree like any other that holds `Klabel`, `Ksnap` and `Kdlist` keys; it is never snapshotted itself.

: `Klabel name[]`
  A label, 1 to 255 bytes.
  The value is `id[8] flags[4]`: the snapshot it names, and flag 1 if the label is a branch, which moves to the branch's new snapshot at every commit.
  A label without it names a snapshot that stays as it is.
: `Ksnap id[8]`
  A snapshot, keyed by its id; the value is 72 bytes:

| Offset | Field | Holds |
| 0 | `root[24]` | a block pointer to the tree's root |
| 24 | `height[4]` | its height, 1 to 32 |
| 28 | `flags[4]` | 0 |
| 32 | `gen[8]` | the id: its newest blocks' generation |
| 40 | `pred[8]` | the one before it on its branch, or 0 |
| 48 | `succ[8]` | the one after it, or 0 |
| 56 | `base[8]` | the snapshot its branch was forked from, or 0 |
| 64 | `nlbl[4]` | the labels naming it |
| 68 | `nref[4]` | the branches forked from it |

: `Kdlist snap[8] birth[8] seq[8]`
  A deadlist: blocks born in generation <birth> that the tree being built as snapshot <snap> no longer reaches, while an older snapshot still does.
  The value is `head[8] count[8]`: the address of the list's first block and how many addresses it holds.
  <seq> is from the superblock's `nextdl`, and keeps apart lists of one snapshot and birth; a deleted snapshot's lists are moved to its successor under new keys, never rewritten.

A snapshot with no label and no fork is deleted.
Deleting snapshot <S>, preceded by <P> and followed by <T>: <S>'s deadlists born at or before <P> become <T>'s; the rest, and <T>'s lists born after <P>, hold blocks only <S> had, and are freed.
Blocks born at or before a branch's `base` are freed by the branch they came from, never by it.

A deadlist block, like a block of the freed chain, is laid out as a log block, type 4, its entries addresses of 8 bytes, at most 2043 to a block; `next`'s address is the next block, and 0 ends the chain.

@node=files title="Files and directories"

A branch's tree, or a snapshot's, holds one file system.
Its root is `Kent(0, "")`, a key of 9 bytes, and every other entry is `Kent(`<pqid>`, `<name>`)` in its directory, so a directory's entries are one range of keys, in name order.
A name is 1 to 247 bytes other than `.` and `..`, with no `/` or NUL.
Every entry, the root's too, has a `Kup` naming its key, so it is found by its qid, and a directory's parent is the `pqid` of its key.
There are no hard links: each entry has one name.

An entry is 77 bytes:

| Offset | Field | Holds |
| 0 | `flags[8]` | 0 |
| 8 | `qid.path[8]` | the file's number, from `nextqid` |
| 16 | `qid.vers[4]` | grows at each change |
| 20 | `qid.type[1]` | 0x80 a directory, 0x02 a link, 0 a file |
| 21 | `mode[4]` | the permission bits and the type, below |
| 25 | `atime[8]` | the last access |
| 33 | `mtime[8]` | the last change of the contents |
| 41 | `ctime[8]` | the last change of the entry |
| 49 | `btime[8]` | the creation |
| 57 | `length[8]` | the bytes, at most 2^62; 0 for a directory |
| 65 | `uid[4]` | the owner |
| 69 | `gid[4]` | the group |
| 73 | `muid[4]` | the last to change the contents |

`mode`'s low 12 bits, 07777, are the permission bits; 0x80000000 marks a directory and 0x02000000 a symbolic link.
`qid.path` is never reused.
Owners are numbers; users(6) names them.

A file's data is a `Kdat` key for each 16384-byte block that holds any; a missing key reads as zeros.
A file of at most 511 bytes is kept whole as inline bytes in `Kdat(`<qid>`, 0)`; a longer one is kept in data blocks, one pointer a block.
A symbolic link is a file whose target, 1 to 511 bytes, is its inline data.

A file removed while it is open loses its `Kent`; its entry moves to `Korphan(`<qid>`)`, its `Kup` names that key, and its data stays until it is freed, when the last open goes or, after a crash, when its branch is next opened.

# EXAMPLES

A new volume, its superblock and its one arena's header:

```rc
% vxfs mkfs t.img 64 adm home
% vxfs info t.img
commit 3, 1 arenas, next generation 10
used 80 of 65472 KiB
branch adm: snapshot 7
branch home: snapshot 8
% od -A d -t x1 -N 136 t.img
0000000 76 78 66 73 01 00 00 00 00 40 00 00 fb 1f 00 00
0000016 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 80 fd 03 00 00 00 00
0000032 99 96 bf 61 b0 55 fb 77 09 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
0000048 03 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 0a 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
0000064 04 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00
0000080 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 80 fe 03 00 00 00 00
0000096 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 40 00 00 00 00 00 00
0000112 fc 0f 00 00 00 00 00 00 f7 ab 14 67 80 50 ad c8
0000128 40 c5 b5 1e df bc b1 11
0000136
% od -A d -t x1 -j 16384 -N 44 t.img
0016384 05 00 00 40 00 00 00 00 00 00 fc 0f 00 00 00 00
0016400 00 00 00 80 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 80 00 00 00 00
0016416 00 00 c8 00 cf 47 aa ef 08 a4 15 de
0016428
```

The superblock: one arena, a snapshot tree of height 1 rooted at 0x3fd8000 and born in generation 9, commit 3, next generation 10, next qid 4 (the two roots and `/adm/users` took 1 to 3), the freed chain at 0x3fe8000, then the arena (its header at 0x4000, 4092 data blocks, its header's hash) and the superblock's hash.
The arena's header: its log is one block, at 0x8000, of which the commit covers 200 bytes.

